Unreleased
==========

* New command minideb mcp: a local Model Context Protocol server for AI agents
  on stdin/stdout (JSON-RPC 2.0, standard library only, Linux and macOS, never
  as root). Tools: repository_overview, search_packages, package_versions,
  package_info, source_info, find_file, appstream_components, download_package,
  inspect_deb and inspect_dsc. Downloads go only to --download-dir (default:
  the current directory) and a private per-session temporary directory, never
  overwrite, never follow symlinks, and are verified against a SHA256 or
  stronger hash from the repository metadata. Options: --download-dir,
  --no-download, --allow-host, --allow-link-local, --max-response-bytes,
  --max-download-bytes, --call-timeout and --download-timeout. Repositories
  come from --url and --sources only (the system sources are not read); the
  agent may also name a repository URL in each call. See "Using minideb with AI
  agents" in README.md, the MCP SERVER section of the man page and the new
  example examples/12_mcp_client.py.
* RepoClient.fetch_dsc() takes an optional keyword-only verify argument that
  overrides the client's setting for that one fetch.
* verify_file() takes an optional keyword-only progress callback that is called
  before every read, so callers can stop a long verification (cancellation,
  deadlines).
* DebPackage.read() takes an optional max_size: a larger member raises
  DebMemberTooLargeError (a DebSizeLimitError) before it is read, and
  DebPackage.files() takes an optional max_entries (DebEntryLimitError, a
  DebSizeLimitError, when the data archive has more entries).
* The Fetcher refuses link-local addresses (169.254.0.0/16, fe80::/10), the
  metadata service addresses fd00:ec2::254 and 100.100.100.200 and the cloud
  metadata host names metadata, metadata.google.internal and instance-data,
  for requested URLs and redirect targets alike (HostNotAllowed, a
  FetchError). Host names are also checked when connecting: a name that
  resolves to such an address is refused, and the connection goes to the
  checked address. Host names are compared in their IDNA form. Pass
  block_link_local=False to allow all of these. Loopback and private
  addresses are still allowed. Through a proxy (http_proxy/https_proxy)
  the proxy resolves the names, so only the URL text is checked; the proxy
  is trusted configuration. IPv6 addresses embedding an IPv4 address
  (IPv4-mapped, NAT64 64:ff9b::/96, IPv4-compatible) are judged by that
  address. The connected peer address is checked again before anything
  is sent.
* Fetcher(allowed_hosts=...) restricts http(s) requests and redirects to the
  given hosts (exact names or *.suffix wildcards; file: URLs are not
  affected). A bare string, an entry with a path or a port, and an entry that
  is neither an IP address nor a host name of letters, digits, "-" and "_"
  labels (after an optional "*.") are rejected. minideb mcp refuses to start
  when a --url or --sources repository is outside --allow-host.
* Fetcher.check_host(url) applies the host policy to a URL without making a
  request (HostNotAllowed when refused), and minideblib.repo.release gains
  is_not_found(err): whether a FetchError means HTTP 404 or a missing
  file:// file.
* Fetcher.get() and Fetcher.exists() take an optional per-call CancelToken
  (token=) and a time.monotonic() deadline (deadline=), and the new
  Fetcher.scope(token=, deadline=) context manager applies them to every
  request inside it, including those of RepoClient (suite probing too).
  They are checked before every attempt and between body chunks, end
  backoff waits early and bound each socket wait (DNS resolution is not
  bounded by the deadline); cancellation raises
  FetchCancelled, an expired deadline the new FetchTimeout. get() and
  exists() gained keyword-only parameters: subclasses that override them
  should accept them (for example with **kw) and pass them on.
* Fetcher.download(url, out, max_bytes=..., expected_size=, expected_hashes=,
  token=, deadline=) streams a body into a binary file object in chunks,
  hashing it on the way (a failed write raises FetchError; out is not
  flushed or closed), and returns a DownloadResult (size and hex
  digests: every requested algorithm plus sha256). Nothing is written
  before the status and headers are checked; max_bytes is enforced through
  Content-Length and while streaming; a size or digest mismatch raises
  HashMismatch (after writing: the caller discards the output). Retries
  happen only before the first byte is written. Token, deadline, scope,
  cancel() and the host policy apply as for get(); file: URLs work too.
* Fetcher refuses file: URLs that do not name a regular file (a FIFO or
  device could block beyond any deadline); symlinks are followed.
* RepoClient.plan_binary() and RepoClient.plan_source() resolve a binary or
  source package to a DownloadPlan of FileSpec items (name, URL, size and
  the strongest listed hash) without downloading anything but indices: one
  plan per architecture for architecture-specific packages, one plan for an
  Architecture: all package listed in several per-arch indices (included
  for any architecture filter; binary-all is read where Release offers
  it), the newest version per target architecture, the .dsc first for
  sources. RepoClient.group_packages() groups records into
  PackageGroup items (one per suite, name, version and component, with
  their architectures, Multi-Arch and per-architecture variants).
* RepoClient.search() gained keyword-only components=, architectures= and
  kind= ("binary" or "source", which returns SourceRecord items) filters;
  the old call form is unchanged.
* RepoClient.problems keeps the newest 1000 messages (problems_dropped
  counts the others); problems_mark() and problems_since(mark) give the
  messages one call added (problems is read-only for callers). The caches
  and the problem list are safe to use from several threads. RepoClient(cache_budget_bytes=...) (default 512
  MiB) bounds the cached parsed indices by an estimate (8 times their
  decompressed size, 4 for Contents), dropping the least recently used ones; they are
  downloaded again when needed.
* minideb -vv prints the traceback of an error after its one-line message,
  and the warnings about malformed one-line sources.list entries have URL
  credentials removed.
* Control data is split at LF only, like apt: one CR before an LF is
  dropped, and a lone CR is no longer a line break (it stays in the value),
  so a value cannot smuggle extra fields or paragraphs past apt's reading.
  The same rule applies to checksum blocks, .dsc Package-List, deb md5sums,
  one-line sources.list files and changelogs (form feeds, U+2028 and other
  separators no longer end a line there). RepoClient records a malformed
  index as a problem and goes on without it instead of raising ControlError.
* A malformed http_proxy/https_proxy setting is reported as "invalid proxy
  setting (http_proxy/https_proxy)" instead of quoting it (with its
  password), and the errors and warnings for malformed sources files have
  URL credentials removed.
* RepoClient records a problem (once) for a malformed checksum field of a
  Sources stanza, which SourceRecord.files then ignores, instead of only
  logging a warning.
* RepoClient(max_index_bytes=...) bounds one decompressed index (default
  1 GiB, as before), and fetch_index() with verification refuses an index
  that Release lists as larger than the Fetcher's max_bytes before
  requesting it. minideb mcp downloads at most 256 MiB per index and
  accepts at most 512 MiB decompressed; one find_file call reads at most
  an estimated 1 GiB of parsed Contents.

minideblib 0.7.0 (2026-11-01)
=============================

A rewrite for current Python and Debian archives. This release breaks
compatibility with 0.6.x.

Breaking changes
----------------

* Python 3.11 or later is required. Python 2 and older Python 3 versions are
  no longer supported.
* Modules were renamed to snake_case under the minideblib package:
  DpkgControl -> control, DpkgChangelog -> changelog, and so on. DpkgDatalist,
  DpkgVarlist, OrderedDict and LoggableObject are gone; the standard
  logging module and dict types are used instead.
* AptRepoClient is replaced by minideblib.repo.client.RepoClient, and
  DpkgDebPackage by minideblib.deb.DebPackage. Both have new interfaces and
  raise typed exceptions (FetchError, DebError, DscError, ...).
* DpkgVersion was reimplemented from the Debian Policy rules as
  minideblib.version.Version (comparison with epochs, tildes and revisions).
  VersionError messages quote at most 40 characters of an invalid version.
  Parsing is permissive like dpkg (an upstream version may start with a
  letter); Version(..., strict=True) and compare(..., strict=True) reject
  an upstream version that does not start with a digit.
* signed.py (SignedFile, DebianSigVerifier, GPGSigVerifier) was removed.
  Signature verification is not provided.
* The modules gpg, safewrite and utils and the ChangeFile class (with
  FileEntry and ChangeFileError) were removed as unused. The .dsc and
  checksum file verification moved to the new minideblib.checksums module
  (parse_multiline_hashes, verify_file, ChecksumError).
* control and checksums are independent implementations. Malformed control
  data raises control.ControlError (a ValueError) whose message starts with
  "line N:"; one leading byte order mark is ignored, and CR, LF and CRLF end
  lines for every kind of input, streams included.
* Checksum handling is stricter. The size column of a checksum block is an
  optional "-" followed by 1 to 20 ASCII digits: "+5", "1_000", non-ASCII
  digits and longer numbers are malformed. A backslash is no longer allowed
  in a .dsc file name. verify_file() raises ChecksumError for an empty or
  unknown algorithm list, reports OS errors during verification as the
  caller's error type (DscError for Dsc.verify), and never follows a
  symlink or blocks on a FIFO or device, even one swapped in after the
  first check.
* Packaging moved from setup.py to pyproject.toml (hatchling); setup.py,
  setup.cfg and the Python 2 files were removed.

New
---

* minideb command line tool: suites, components, archs, list, show, versions,
  search, files, appstream, icons, source, dsc and deb commands, short aliases,
  --json output, -v/-vv logging, --probe, global -c/-a scoping, and a
  self-contained minideb.pyz. Exit status 1 means nothing found (show,
  files, versions, source, suites, appstream --id/--package, deb field);
  list and search exit with 0 on an empty result. Index records with an
  invalid Version are skipped with a warning.
  The pyz build honours SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH within the zip timestamp range
  (1980-01-01 to 2107-12-31); other values stop the build with a one-line
  error.
* -c/-a narrow the configured sources entries: with --sources, "list -c X"
  for a suite whose entries do not list component X now gives an empty
  result and a warning (previously X was queried anyway); use
  "--url URL list -c X" to query a component that is not configured. A
  scoped architecture that Release does not list is reported as "not listed
  in Release" rather than as scoped away.
* Sources entries for the same URI and suite are merged; an entry without
  components (or without an architecture restriction) keeps the merged
  entry unrestricted, so "deb URI suite" plus "deb URI suite main" queries
  every component listed in Release.
* Release-driven repository discovery: no directory listings are needed,
  suites containing "/" work, and file hashes from Release are verified.
  SHA256 or stronger is required: indices or .dsc files listed only with
  MD5/SHA-1 are rejected unless verification is turned off (--no-verify).
* Fetching refuses https -> http redirects, credentials in URLs (also in
  redirect targets) and ".." components in file:// URLs (also
  percent-encoded).
* Repository paths from Release, Packages (Filename) and Sources
  (Directory, file names) and suite names are checked before a URL is
  built: a ".." segment, a leading "/", a backslash or NUL is refused also
  when percent-encoded (repeatedly decoded, e.g. %2e%2e or %252e%252e), and
  so are encoded separators (%2f, %5c, %00). Other escapes such as %2B or
  %3a stay valid. An unsafe or empty suite raises ReleaseError (naming at
  most 40 characters of it) without any request; sources entries are
  checked when used, not when parsed.
* deb822 .sources files and one-line sources.list entries. One-line
  entries may separate their fields with any mix of spaces and tabs (also
  around the [options] block).
* Acquire-By-Hash and flat repositories.
* Packages, Sources and Contents indices; DEP-11 AppStream metadata and icons
  (optional PyYAML).
  A Contents line is split into path and location on spaces and tabs
  only; other whitespace (U+00A0, form feed, ...) belongs to the path.
* Source packages: .dsc parsing, hash-checked download and verification.
  SourceRecord.files lists each file with the strongest hash the Sources
  stanza gives for it (Checksums-Sha512, -Sha256, -Sha1, then Files), the
  same order fetch_dsc verifies in.
* Compression: gzip, bzip2, xz, lzma and zstd (native on Python 3.14+,
  otherwise the optional zstandard).
  The output size limit (max_size) also applies to uncompressed data.
  gzip input must contain at least one complete member: empty or zero-only
  data and a truncated first member raise DecompressError; zero padding
  after a complete member is still ignored.
  The xz/lzma decoder runs with a memory limit (max_size clamped to
  128 MiB .. 1 GiB): a stream whose header asks for a larger dictionary
  raises DecompressionLimitError instead of allocating it.
* Safe .deb extraction (path, link and size checks) and a dpkg-deb style
  subset: -I, -e, -c, -x, -f.
  An ar archive whose odd-sized last member lacks its padding byte is
  rejected as truncated (ArError).
  The debian-binary member must hold format 2.x (any minor version);
  other formats and malformed content raise DebError.
  "minideb deb ctrl", "minideb deb extract" and DebPackage.extract() refuse
  a destination directory that is a symlink; "deb ctrl" does not follow
  symlinks below it (a symlinked file or directory is refused) and replaces
  existing regular files instead of writing into them. Only the last
  component of the destination is checked: symlinks in the directories
  leading to it are followed (they are the caller's choice).
  DebPackage.extract() resolves relative symlink targets up front, following
  the archive's own symlinks, and rejects a link that would point outside
  the destination before anything is written.
* minideblib.MinidebError, the common base class of the exceptions the
  library raises for bad data: malformed control data, indices, Release,
  changelogs (changelog.ChangelogError), sources files
  (sources.SourcesError, also for a file that is not valid UTF-8), .deb and
  .dsc files, versions, compressed data and DEP-11 YAML, unusable index
  records (repo.client.RepoError), failed downloads and hash checks. The
  concrete classes keep their other bases such as ValueError;
  DebPackage.read() raises deb.MissingMemberError, a DebError and KeyError.
  Not covered: programmer errors (wrong argument types, an unknown probe
  set or index kind), OSError from local file access, KeyError for a
  missing Paragraph field and errors raised by caller-supplied objects.
* Type annotations and py.typed; runnable examples in examples/.
* Native Debian packaging, an RPM spec for Fedora and openSUSE, a man page, CI
  and tag-driven releases. The workflows pin every action to a commit SHA;
  Dependabot proposes weekly pin updates.

Fixed
-----

Issues of the old GitHub tracker:

* #1 DpkgSourceControl choked on DOS newlines: control files with CRLF line
  endings are parsed (regression test).
* #2 DpkgParagraph.load() truncated the last character of every line: fixed
  (regression test).
* #3 multi-line fields such as Binary are now available as folded text or
  lists via Paragraph.get_folded() / get_list(); covered by the .dsc
  wrapped-Binary tests.
* #4 network errors are retried: Fetcher retries transient failures
  (timeouts, 5xx, 429, truncated bodies) with backoff and keeps the last
  status in FetchError (regression tests). Fetcher.cancel() stops the
  retries of the requests in progress (they raise FetchCancelled, a
  FetchError); the backoff waits are interruptible.
* Backoff waits are controlled with Fetcher(sleep=...) and Fetcher.cancel();
  patching time.sleep no longer has an effect.
* Ctrl-C while RepoClient.suites() probes suites returns at once: probes run
  on daemon threads, queued probes are dropped and in-flight retries are
  cancelled, so neither the call nor interpreter exit waits for them.

Known limitations
-----------------

* The Release/InRelease signature is not verified; only the hashes listed in
  Release are checked. See TODO.md.
* DebPackage.extract() and "minideb deb extract" refuse symlinks with an
  absolute target (fail closed), so many real packages are refused by
  default. skip_absolute_symlinks=True / --skip-absolute-symlinks leaves
  such links out (one warning each) and extracts the rest; other unsafe
  members, and hard links to a skipped symlink, are still refused. Hard
  links must point to an earlier regular member and member mtimes must be
  finite and in [0, 2**37); DebPackage.files() and "minideb deb ls" list an
  invalid mtime as 0.

Older releases
--------------

See debian/changelog for the history of the 0.6.x series.
